 ],
 "literal": "Closely-knit garland-wearing, bamboo-like large shoulders-having women, / sea-bathing women, in the seaside grove built / small toy-house, the friendship that was formed there, O friend! / If for one day the lord of the shore abandons us, / for many days, pain will come.",
 "translation": "O friend! The love that was formed in the small toy-houses built in the seaside groves by sea-bathing women with thick garlands and large, bamboo-like shoulders—if the lord of the shore leaves us for even a single day, it brings many days of pain.",
 "modern": "தோழி! நெருக்கமாகத் தொடுக்கப்பட்ட மாலைகளையும், மூங்கில் போன்ற பெரிய தோள்களையும் உடைய, கடலில் நீராடும் பெண்கள் கடற்கரைச் சோலையில் கட்டிய சிறு மணல் வீட்டில் உண்டான நம் நட்பு போன்றது இது. இத்துறைமுகத் தலைவன் நம்மை ஒரு நாள் பிரிந்தாலும், அது பல நாட்களுக்குத் தொடரும் துன்பத்தைத் தருவதாகும்.",
 "explanation": "The heroine (Thalaivi) is speaking to her close friend (Thōḻi) about her deep distress over the brief absence of her lover. She compares their love to the play-houses built on the shore. Just as children build small sandcastles in the seaside groves, their love began in innocent play but has now become deep and binding. This poem belongs to the Neytal tiṇai (maritime landscape), characterized by themes of waiting, anxiety, and the pain of separation. The imagery of sea-bathing women (kaṭalāṭu makaḷir) and seaside groves (kāṉal) perfectly evokes the coastal setting. Key words include tuṟaivaṉ (lord of the shore), naṭpu (friendship/love), and iṉṉāmaittē (it is of a painful nature).",
